</ol>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/15.E:_Equilibria_of_Other_Reaction_Classes_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_165"><span id="S15.3.2_13"></span><h3 id="S15.3.2-46161">S15.3.2</h3>

<p><em>K</em><sub>sp</sub> = [Mg<sup>2+</sup>][F<sup>&ndash;</sup>]<sup>2</sup> = (1.21 &times; 10<sup>&ndash;3</sup>)(2 &times; 1.21 &times; 10<sup>&ndash;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = 7.09 &times; 10<sup>&ndash;9</sup>; 7.09 &times; 10<sup>&ndash;7</sup> <em>M</em></p>

<p>Determine the concentration of Mg<sup>2+</sup> and F<sup>&ndash;</sup> that will be present in the final volume. Compare the value of the ion product [Mg<sup>2+</sup>][F<sup>&ndash;</sup>]<sup>2</sup> with <em>K</em><sub>sp</sub>. If this value is larger than <em>K</em><sub>sp</sub>, precipitation will occur.   0.1000 L &times; 3.00 &times; 10<sup>&ndash;3</sup> <em>M</em> Mg(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> = 0.3000 L &times; <em>M</em> Mg(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>   <em>M</em> Mg(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> = 1.00 &times; 10<sup>&ndash;3</sup> <em>M</em>   0.2000 L &times; 2.00 &times; 10<sup>&ndash;3</sup> <em>M</em> NaF = 0.3000 L &times; <em>M</em> NaF   <em>M</em> NaF = 1.33 &times; 10<sup>&ndash;3</sup> <em>M</em>   ion product = (1.00 &times; 10<sup>&ndash;3</sup>)(1.33 &times; 10<sup>&ndash;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = 1.77 &times; 10<sup>&ndash;9</sup>   This value is smaller than <em>K</em><sub>sp</sub>, so no precipitation will occur.   MgF<sub>2</sub> is less soluble at 27 &deg;C than at 18 &deg;C. Because added heat acts like an added reagent, when it appears on the product side, the Le Chatelier&rsquo;s principle states that the equilibrium will shift to the reactants&rsquo; side to counter the stress. Consequently, less reagent will dissolve. This situation is found in our case. Therefore, the reaction is exothermic.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/15.E:_Equilibria_of_Other_Reaction_Classes_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_166"><span id="Q15.3.X_14"></span><h3 id="Q15.3.X-46161"><span>Q15.3.X</span></h3>

<p>Which of the following compounds, when dissolved in a 0.01-<em>M</em> solution of HClO<sub>4</sub>, has a solubility greater than in pure water: CuCl, CaCO<sub>3</sub>, MnS, PbBr<sub>2</sub>, CaF<sub>2</sub>? Explain your answer.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/15.E:_Equilibria_of_Other_Reaction_Classes_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_167"><span id="Q15.3.X_15"></span><h3 id="Q15.3.X-46161"><span>Q15.3.X</span></h3>

<p>Which of the following compounds, when dissolved in a 0.01-<em>M</em> solution of HClO<sub>4</sub>, has a solubility greater than in pure water: AgBr, BaF<sub>2</sub>, Ca<sub>3</sub>(PO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>3</sub>, ZnS, PbI<sub>2</sub>? Explain your answer.</p>

<p>BaF<sub>2</sub>, Ca<sub>3</sub>(PO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>, ZnS; each is a salt of a weak acid, and the \(\ce{[H3O+]}\) from perchloric acid reduces the equilibrium concentration of the anion, thereby increasing the concentration of the cations</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/15.E:_Equilibria_of_Other_Reaction_Classes_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_168"><span id="Q15.3.X_16"></span><h3 id="Q15.3.X-46161"><span>Q15.3.X</span></h3>

<p>What is the effect on the amount of solid Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> that dissolves and the concentrations of Mg<sup>2+</sup> and OH<sup>&ndash;</sup> when each of the following are added to a mixture of solid Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> and water at equilibrium?</p>

<ol start="1" style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
    <li>MgCl<sub>2</sub></li>
    <li>KOH</li>
    <li>HClO<sub>4</sub></li>
    <li>NaNO<sub>3</sub></li>
    <li>Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub></li>
</ol>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/15.E:_Equilibria_of_Other_Reaction_Classes_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_169"><span id="Q15.3.X_17"></span><h3 id="Q15.3.X-46161"><span>Q15.3.X</span></h3>

<p>What is the effect on the amount of CaHPO<sub>4</sub> that dissolves and the concentrations of Ca<sup>2+</sup> and \(\ce{HPO4-}\) when each of the following are added to a mixture of solid CaHPO<sub>4</sub> and water at equilibrium?</p>

<ol start="1" style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
    <li>CaCl<sub>2</sub></li>
    <li>HCl</li>
    <li>KClO<sub>4</sub></li>
    <li>NaOH</li>
    <li>CaHPO<sub>4</sub></li>
</ol>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/15.E:_Equilibria_of_Other_Reaction_Classes_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_170"><span id="S15.3.X"></span><h3 id="S15.3.X-46161"><span>S15.3.X</span></h3>

<p>Effect on amount of solid CaHPO<sub>4</sub>, [Ca<sup>2+</sup>], [OH<sup>&ndash;</sup>]: increase, increase, decrease; decrease, increase, decrease; no effect, no effect, no effect; decrease, increase, decrease; increase, no effect, no effect</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/15.E:_Equilibria_of_Other_Reaction_Classes_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_171"><span id="Q15.3.X_18"></span><h3 id="Q15.3.X-46161"><span>Q15.3.X</span></h3>

<p>Identify all chemical species present in an aqueous solution of Ca<sub>3</sub>(PO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub> and list these species in decreasing order of their concentrations. (Hint: Remember that the \(\ce{PO4^3-}\) ion is a weak base.)</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/15.E:_Equilibria_of_Other_Reaction_Classes_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_172"><span id="Q15.3.X_19"></span><h3 id="Q15.3.X-46161"><span>Q15.3.X</span></h3>

<p>A volume of 50 mL of 1.8 <em>M</em> NH<sub>3</sub> is mixed with an equal volume of a solution containing 0.95 g of MgCl<sub>2</sub>. What mass of NH<sub>4</sub>Cl must be added to the resulting solution to prevent the precipitation of Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub>?</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/15.E:_Equilibria_of_Other_Reaction_Classes_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_173"><span id="S15.3.X_2"></span><h3 id="S15.3.X-46161"><span>S15.3.X</span></h3>

